# Hermite's identity in closed (src/closed.c): a value rounded down T
# times, each time against another of the T offsets j/T, adds up to the
# value rounded once, T times finer.  It is why a rounding by a counter
# or by a hash of one loses nothing over a whole period.

# the offsets in order
h8(x: real in 0..10) = sum(i, 0, 7, floor(x + real(i)/8))
closed h8
# any T consecutive indices; the first offset moves into the floor
h16(x: real in -3..3) = sum(i, 5, 20, floor(x + real(i)/16))
closed h16
# the offsets by a hash of the index that is a bijection of 0..255: an
# 8-bit store accumulated 256 times keeps the value to 1/256 of a step
hh(x: real in 0..1) = sum(i, 0, 255, floor(255x + real((i*37 + 11) & 255)/256))
closed hh
# the offset as a multiple of 1/T, the hash an xor
hx(x: real in 0..1) = sum(i, 0, 15, floor(16x + 0.0625*real(i xor 5)))
closed hx
# against rounding to nearest every time, at x = 0.123: 8029 by the
# hash, 7936 by rounding, for 8029.44
show hh(0.123)
rn(x: real in 0..1) = sum(i, 0, 255, floor(255x + 0.5))
closed rn
show rn(0.123)
show 256*255*0.123

# not one period: an offset twice, a period cut short, a period that is
# not a power of two (its offsets are not exact in binary)
hb(x: real in 0..1) = sum(i, 0, 255, floor(255x + real((i*2) & 255)/256))
closed hb
h7(x: real in 0..10) = sum(i, 0, 6, floor(x + real(i)/8))
closed h7
h5(x: real in 0..1) = sum(i, 0, 4, floor(255x + real(i)/5))
closed h5
